.TH CLOG_OPEN_FD_MP 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_open_fd_mp \- create a logger writing to an existing file descriptor
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clogger.h>

clog clog_open_fd_mp(int fd, clog_level_t min_level,
                  const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg,
                  ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new logger that writes to an already-open file descriptor.
The file descriptor is not owned by the logger and will NOT be closed
when clog_close() is called. Log rotation is unavailable for fd-backed
loggers.

Use fd=1 for stdout, fd=2 for stderr, or any other open writable fd,
including a pipe or a connected socket (see clog_set_format(3) for the
CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG case). From the moment the first logger of any kind is
created in the process, SIGPIPE is ignored; if fd's peer end later
goes away, a subsequent write is dropped silently instead of
terminating the process.

fd may be opened in either blocking or non-blocking mode. If fd is
non-blocking and a write would otherwise block (EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK),
the calling thread waits for fd to become writable again and retries,
rather than dropping the remainder of the record; a record is only
ever dropped on a genuinely unrecoverable write error.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
fd         Open, writable file descriptor. Must be >= 0.
min_level  Messages below this level are silently dropped. Use
           CLOG_OFF to suppress all output.
async_cfg  Async logging configuration, or NULL for synchronous
           logging. When non-NULL, log_*() calls only format their
           own message and hand the rest off to a dedicated writer
           thread; see clog_async_cfg_t below. Unlike a rotation
           config, an all-zero struct still enables async mode (with
           every zero field falling back to its own default); only a
           literal NULL keeps logging synchronous.
             clog_async_cfg_t fields:
               queue_size        - message-queue capacity; 0 means an
                                    unbounded queue that never blocks
                                    a caller on send; > 0 means a
                                    bounded queue of this many
                                    messages, where a full queue
                                    BLOCKS the caller until space
                                    frees up (never drops a message)
               flush_buffer_size - bytes; the writer thread flushes
                                    once its aggregation buffer
                                    reaches this size; 0 ->
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_BUFFER_SIZE.
                                    A value above the library's own
                                    internal 16 MiB per-record cap is
                                    honored: the aggregation buffer's
                                    own growth ceiling follows it.
               flush_interval_ms - milliseconds; the writer thread
                                    flushes at least this often even
                                    if flush_buffer_size has not been
                                    reached; 0 ->
                                    CLOG_DEFAULT_ASYNC_FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS
mprocs     Custom allocator (malloc/free/calloc/realloc function
           pointers), or NULL to use the default system allocator.
           All four pointers must be set when mprocs is non-NULL;
           a partially-populated struct causes the call to return
           CLOG_INVALID without creating a logger. The struct is
           copied internally; the caller may free it after this call.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new logger handle on success.
Returns CLOG_INVALID if fd < 0, if mprocs is invalid, or if memory
allocation fails.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The fd is not duplicated; the caller is responsible for keeping it
open for the lifetime of the logger.
All public functions are fully thread-safe via a per-logger mutex.
A handle open at the time of fork() remains fully usable in the
child: logging, deriving, and closing it all continue to work
correctly on both sides of the fork. A logger's async writer thread
does not exist in a forked child; an async-enabled handle inherited
into a child transparently falls back to synchronous logging there.

When async_cfg is non-NULL, CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG output is exempt from
batching regardless of queue_size/flush_buffer_size/flush_interval_ms:
every record and every backtrace frame still keeps its own write()
call. A CLOG_FATAL call always bypasses the queue: it drains
everything already queued from earlier calls, then writes the fatal
record itself synchronously, before terminating the process.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_close (3),
.BR clog_flush (3),
.BR log_info (3),
.BR log_error (3)
